my first job out of college was working  at a group home for at-risk teenagers I  mean it was the craziest thing that I've  ever done and seeing firsthand the  effects of bad parenting I was forced to  think about questions that I've never  thought about before and a lot of those  questions stuck with me all the way  through my my time at film school my  caesars project was a short film called  short term 12 the film premiered at  sundance and we ended up winning the  jury prize there that was the first time  that I realized how universal this story  is and I knew there was just so much  more to tell about this world so it  started the long process of trying to  write the future screenplay and then  beginning of 2010 I submitted a draft to  the nickel fellowship I submitted and I  didn't think anything of it and then I  got that first email that we got into  the quarterfinals and then I just kept  going until finally I got the call I was  trying to say something but like nothing  was coming out of my mouth and I can't  even describe what it feels like to have  any kind of nod from the Academy it just  feels like a warm hug  I mean there's so many best parts of the  nickel experience it's not just winning  it it's also this continuing support and  love from from a community that is so  open and really does feel like a family  yeah I met some of my best friends  through the nickel fellowship so it's in  a weird way I'd like to thank the  academy for my friends the year that I  won the fellowship Michael Arndt gave  the speech of that night and it was  hands down the best speech I've ever  heard in my life the words that were  coming out of his mouth were were rooted  in humility and I think the the best  piece of advice that I absorbed from who  he is is to always have a a grateful and  thankful attitude toward any of this I  mean it's crazy that we're able to  continue to tell stories and play  make-believe for a living you know  wonderful
 

